\section{Where an intercept prior lands}{

brms centers its design matrix and constrains the intercept at the
MEAN of the predictors, recovering the reported one as
\code{b_Intercept = Intercept - dot_product(means_X, b)}. frmtmb
parameterizes by the intercept at zero and evaluates a class
\code{"Intercept"} density at \verb{b0 + means_X'b}, so the two packages
constrain the same quantity. The map between the two
parameterizations is unit triangular and carries no Jacobian.

This matters whenever a predictor is not centered. On
\code{Reaction ~ Days}, where \code{mean(Days)} is 4.5, the intercept at zero
is strongly correlated with the slope and the intercept at the mean
is orthogonal to it, so a prior on the first biases the slope and a
prior on the second does not. Earlier releases used the intercept
at zero, and \code{brms::get_prior()}'s own default there moved the slope
by 0.068 standard errors; it now moves it by 0.00003.

Every sub-formula with an intercept is centered separately, as brms
does (\code{means_X}, \code{means_X_sigma}, ...). A NONLINEAR parameter's
sub-formula is not centered on either side, and neither are a
smooth's unpenalized columns or a \code{mo()} term, which sit outside
brms's \code{Xc} as well. To put a density on the intercept at zero, name
it as a coefficient instead: \verb{class = "b", coef = "Intercept"},
which is also how a \code{brms::bf(center = FALSE)} model's prior arrives.
}

\section{Ordinal thresholds}{

\code{cumulative()}, \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()} and \code{acat()} have no intercept
column: the thresholds replace it. brms priors them as its
\code{Intercept} class and so does frmtmb, so
\code{set_prior("student_t(3, 0, 2.5)", class = "Intercept")} on an
ordinal model addresses the whole threshold vector. It addresses the
THRESHOLDS, at the mean of the predictors, with the log-Jacobian of
the map from frmtmb's internal storage; \code{cumulative()} and
\code{sratio()} hold \verb{(tau_1, log increments)}, which is the same map
Stan's \code{ordered} type applies, and \code{cratio()} and \code{acat()} hold the
thresholds themselves. \code{lb}/\code{ub} are refused there, because one
number cannot box a whole vector of ordered thresholds.

\code{prior = list(tau_raw = prior_normal(0, 5))} reaches the same
parameters on the INTERNAL scale, one entry per threshold, which is
the escape hatch to use when the increments rather than the
thresholds are what a prior is about.
}

\section{Hard bounds}{

\code{lb}/\code{ub} are how a box constraint is written. A specification may
carry bounds alone (\code{prior = ""}), a distribution alone, or both, and
a later bounds-only specification tightens an entry an earlier
distribution created rather than replacing it.

A bound is addressed exactly like the distribution beside it, so
\code{set_prior("", nlpar = "guess", lb = 0, ub = 1)} bounds the nonlinear
parameter \code{guess}, and \code{dpar}, \code{resp}, \code{group} and \code{coef} narrow a
bound the same way they narrow a density. As with a distribution,
class \code{"b"} with \code{nlpar} covers EVERY coefficient of that parameter;
\code{coef} picks out one.

The scale is the parameter's own: class \code{"sd"} bounds a standard
deviation on the sd scale (frmtmb stores its log), classes \code{"ar"},
\code{"ma"} and \code{"cosy"} bound the natural coefficient of a residual
structure, and class \code{"theta"} bounds an internal covariance
parameter itself, one position at a time with \code{coef = "theta_2"},
\code{"thetaac_1"} or \code{"thetar_1"}. Everything else is bounded on the
internal (link) scale, so a bound on a log-linked dispersion is a
bound on its logarithm.

In \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}} a bound is a box constraint handed to the optimizer; in
\code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} it becomes one of Stan's constrained
transforms. Both take this spelling and no other: the \code{lower}/\code{upper}
arguments of releases before 0.49 are gone rather than aliased, and a
call still using them fails as an unused argument. Every outer
parameter they could reach has a class here, down to a single
internal covariance parameter.

Where the two spellings differed, this one broadcasts: a bound
carried by \verb{nlpar =} covers every coefficient of that parameter, the
way a prior does, and \code{coef} narrows it to one. When two
specifications bound the same parameter the later one wins, so a
bounds-only specification after a wide one tightens it.
}

\section{Residual correlation}{

frmtmb holds an \code{ar()}, \code{ma()}, \code{arma()}, \code{cosy()} or \code{unstr()}
residual block in one unconstrained vector, chosen so the optimizer
cannot step outside the stationary and invertible region. A prior is
still written about the parameter brms names, and carried onto that
vector with the log Jacobian of the map, exactly as class \code{"sd"}
carries a density on a standard deviation onto its logarithm. So
\code{set_prior("normal(0, 0.5)", class = "ar")} is a density on the AR
coefficient itself, and \code{summary()} reports the parameter the prior
was written about.

Bounds behave the same way where the map allows it. A first-order
\code{ar}, \code{ma} or \code{cosy} coefficient is a monotone function of one
internal parameter, so \code{lb}/\code{ub} map exactly onto a box. At order two
and above they do not: \code{ar[1]} is a function of every internal
parameter of the block at once, so no box in internal space is the
box asked for, and \code{lb}/\code{ub} are refused rather than approximated.
Little is lost, because the parameterization already guarantees
stationarity and invertibility, which is what such a bound is usually
for; where a hard box really is wanted, \code{class = "theta"} with
\code{coef = "thetaac_1"} bounds one internal parameter.

\code{cosy} is bounded below at \code{-1/(d - 1)} for \code{d} time points, where a
compound-symmetric matrix stops being positive definite, and a bound
outside that window is refused rather than clamped. brms bounds
\code{cosy} on \verb{[0, 1]} instead, so a negative estimate here has no brms
counterpart.
}

\section{The LKJ prior}{

\code{lkj(eta)} is the density \code{det(C)^(eta - 1)} over a block's
correlation matrix \code{C}, normalized: \code{eta = 1} is uniform over
correlation matrices, larger \code{eta} concentrates toward the identity.
frmtmb holds a correlation as an unconstrained row-normalized
Cholesky parameter rather than as \code{C}, so the density is carried onto
those parameters with the exact Jacobian of that map (the derivation
is in the source of \code{R/priors.R}; \code{tests/testthat/test-lkj.R} checks
the sampled correlations against the closed-form LKJ marginals). The
prior a FLAT correlation parameter carries instead is
\code{(1 - rho^2)^(-3/2)}, which is improper.

It fits \code{us()} and \code{gr(cov = )} blocks of two or more terms, which
hold a whole correlation matrix, and the one-parameter structures
\code{cs()}, \code{ar1()} and \code{hetar1()}, whose single bounded correlation
takes the LKJ marginal \code{(1 - rho^2)^(eta - 1)} with that structure's
own Jacobian. A \code{cs()} correlation is bounded below at \code{-1/(d - 1)},
where a compound-symmetric matrix stops being positive definite, and
the density is renormalized over that window. \code{toep()} is refused:
its parameterization is not positive definite everywhere, so it has
no correlation matrix to put a density on.
}

\section{Nonlinear parameters}{

\code{nlpar} addresses one parameter of an \code{nl = TRUE} formula, brms's
spelling: \code{set_prior("normal(5000, 1000)", nlpar = "ult")}, or
\code{prior(normal(5000, 1000), nlpar = "ult")}. Class \code{"b"} there covers
EVERY coefficient of that parameter, its intercept included, because
a nonlinear parameter's sub-formula is not centered and brms holds
its intercept in the same coefficient vector as its slopes. That is
why the vignette spelling above lands on \code{ult_(Intercept)} rather
than on nothing. Narrow to one column with \code{coef} (\code{"Intercept"} and
\code{"(Intercept)"} both name the intercept), or write
\verb{class = "Intercept", nlpar = "ult"}, which is frmtmb's spelling of
the same slot. \code{nlpar} narrows classes \code{"sd"} and \code{"cor"} to the
random-effect blocks of that parameter as well.

A prior with a location places \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}}'s \code{start} for a nonlinear
parameter. \code{normal()}, \code{student_t()} and \code{cauchy()} all carry one,
and where \code{start} does not set a nonlinear coefficient, that
coefficient begins at the prior's location, reported in a message.
Other parameters keep their usual starts: a prior is a penalty, not
a claim about where to begin. Without a located prior a nonlinear
model still needs \code{start}, because \code{frm()} evaluates the objective
AT the starting values; \code{\link[=par_template]{par_template()}} names them.

\code{resp} picks one response of a multivariate model; the default
priors of \code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} still stay off there (see
its Default priors section), so a multivariate model's priors are the
ones
written by hand.
}

\section{Translating a brms prior}{

\code{frm(prior = )} takes a \code{brmsprior} object directly, whether it came
from \code{brms::set_prior()}, \code{brms::prior()} or \code{brms::get_prior()}. A
row applies whatever its \code{prior} string says, which is brms's own
rule; an empty string is brms's flat default and applies nothing.
The \code{source} column is not read. Earlier releases dropped rows marked
\code{source == "default"} were dropped, which lost a prior the user had
edited into a \code{get_prior()} table in place, because brms does not
update \code{source} after that edit.

brms's class vocabulary is wider than the one above, and it is
carried over rather than refused wherever the two packages mean the
same parameter:
\itemize{
\item \code{b}, \code{Intercept}, \code{sd}, \code{cor}, \code{ar}, \code{ma}, \code{cosy}, \code{cortime} and
\code{rescor} are frmtmb's own class names and keep their meaning.
\item a DISTRIBUTIONAL parameter's own class (\code{sigma}, \code{shape}, \code{phi},
\code{nu}, \code{kappa}, \code{sigma1}, ...) is a density on that parameter
ITSELF in brms. It lands on the parameter itself here too, through
the dpar's inverse link with that map's log-Jacobian, which is the
change of variables class \code{"sd"} performs. A bound on such a row
travels with it, so brms's \code{lb = 0} on a log-linked dispersion
becomes no constraint rather than a floor of 1.
\item \code{theta}/\code{theta1}/\code{theta2}, \code{simo}, \code{sds}, \code{sdgp}, \code{lscale},
\code{sdcar} and \code{car} are refused by name, each saying where frmtmb
keeps that quantity instead. A refusal is deliberate: translating
one of them would produce a different model rather than no model.
\item a \code{coef} on a \code{sd} or \code{cor} row is refused for the same reason.
brms narrows such a row to one coefficient of a block; frmtmb
resolves those classes per BLOCK, so applying the row without its
\code{coef} would put the density on every standard deviation of the
block, which is a wider prior than the one written. \code{coef} on
class \code{"b"} narrows as it does in brms and is unaffected.
}

Every refused row of a table is named in ONE message, because a table
is edited as a whole and stopping at the first bad row costs a round
trip per bad row.

\strong{frmtmb's own spelling for a distributional parameter is the one
place the two packages still differ.}
\code{set_prior("student_t(3, 0, 2.5)", class = "Intercept", dpar = "sigma")} is a density on LOG sigma, the parameter frmtmb stores,
and it keeps that meaning; the same row spelled brms's way,
\code{brms::prior(student_t(3, 0, 2.5), class = "sigma")}, is a density on
sigma. The two are not the same prior: on a nonlinear fit with
\code{sigma} near 0.135 the link spelling captures about 35 percent of the
shift the natural one produces, and on \code{sleepstudy}, where brms's
default scale is calibrated to a sigma near 31, it captures none of
it. Write the brms spelling when brms's meaning is wanted.

brms's \code{tag} and \code{check} have no counterpart: \code{tag} names a prior
for reuse inside a Stan program, and \code{check} passes an unchecked
string through to one. frmtmb compiles no Stan program, so both are
omitted rather than accepted and ignored.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(100), z = rnorm(100),
                 g = factor(rep(1:10, 10)))
dd$y <- rnorm(100, 1 + 0.5 * dd$x + rnorm(10, 0, 0.8)[dd$g], 1)

# `+` combines specifications; the class-wide one goes first so the
# coefficient-specific one can override it
pr <- set_prior("normal(0, 1)", class = "b") +
  set_prior("normal(0, 0.2)", class = "b", coef = "z") +
  set_prior("exponential(1)", class = "sd", group = "g")
pr

# the priors penalize the likelihood: the fit is a MAP estimate
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + z + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd, prior = pr)
fixef(fit)$mu
# the tight prior on z shrinks it toward zero
fixef(frm(bf(y ~ x + z + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd))$mu

# an empty distribution string sets a hard bound only
set_prior("", class = "b", coef = "x", lb = 0)

# bounds address a nonlinear parameter the way a distribution does,
# so a guessing rate is held in [0, 1]
set_prior("", nlpar = "guess", lb = 0, ub = 1)

# the residual-correlation classes are brms's own names
set_prior("normal(0, 0.5)", class = "ar")
set_prior("lkj(2)", class = "rescor")
# and one internal covariance parameter, by its name
set_prior("", class = "theta", coef = "thetaac_1", lb = -2, ub = 2)

# class "cor" addresses a correlated block as a whole, brms's
# spelling; eta > 1 pulls the correlation toward zero
dd$z <- rnorm(100)
dd$y2 <- dd$y + rnorm(10, 0, 0.6)[dd$g] * dd$z
fitc <- frm(bf(y2 ~ x + z + (z | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd,
            prior = set_prior("lkj(4)", class = "cor"))
VarCorr(fitc)

# get_prior() shows which rows a design offers
get_prior(bf(y ~ x + z + (1 | g)) + gaussian(), data = dd)

# prior() quotes its first argument, brms's spelling, and reaches
# the same machinery
prior(normal(0, 1), class = "b")
}
